chunks.concept:
The **internal energy** of a substance is the **total** of two things:

- the random kinetic energy of *all* its particles, plus
- the potential energy stored in the forces between them.

Unlike temperature (an average per particle), internal energy is a grand total — so it grows with the amount of substance. The pool has more internal energy than the coffee even though it is cooler.

**Thermal equilibrium** is the state two objects reach when they are at the **same temperature**, so there is **no net transfer of energy** between them.

When a hot object and a cold object are placed in contact:

- energy flows from the hot object to the cold object (always hot → cold);
- the hot object loses internal energy and **cools**;
- the cold object gains internal energy and **warms**;
- this continues until both are at the **same temperature** — thermal equilibrium — after which their temperatures stop changing.
